WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.788
Injection happens when untrusted input changes the meaning of something the application

00:00:04.788 --> 00:00:07.522
sends to an interpreter or downstream system.

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:12.601
That something might be a database query, command, document, template, script, URL, header,

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:14.554
protocol message, or API request.

00:00:14.554 --> 00:00:18.461
The security failure is not simply that input was accepted.

00:00:18.461 --> 00:00:23.149
The failure is that data was allowed to become instructions or structure

00:00:23.149 --> 00:00:26.664
in a context where it should have remained data.

00:00:26.664 --> 00:00:27.836
JavaScript and Node.

00:00:27.836 --> 00:00:29.790
js applications encounter many interpreters.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:34.477
A backend may build SQL queries, NoSQL queries, shell commands, template output,

00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:38.384
JSON documents, URLs, HTTP headers, and messages to downstream APIs.

00:00:38.384 --> 00:00:43.853
A frontend may render HTML, set attributes, build URLs, update the DOM, parse JSON,

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:46.197
and interact with browser security rules.

00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:51.666
Each context has its own rules for what characters, types, structures, and APIs mean.

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:54.401
This is why injection prevention is context-specific.

00:00:54.401 --> 00:00:58.698
Input validation checks whether data has the shape the application expects.

00:00:58.698 --> 00:01:01.433
Parameterization keeps values separate from query structure.

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:05.339
Output encoding makes data safe for a specific rendering context.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:09.636
Escaping may be useful only when it matches the exact interpreter.

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:12.761
Safe API design avoids string-built instructions when possible.

00:01:12.761 --> 00:01:16.277
These controls are related, but they are not interchangeable.

00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:21.746
A common mistake is trying to memorize dangerous inputs instead of understanding sensitive sinks.

00:01:21.746 --> 00:01:27.216
The safer mental model is to ask where untrusted data enters, where it flows,

00:01:27.216 --> 00:01:29.169
and where it is interpreted.

00:01:29.169 --> 00:01:34.638
If the data reaches a database, shell, template engine, browser, file path, URL, header,

00:01:34.638 --> 00:01:39.716
or external API, the developer must choose a control appropriate to that destination.

00:01:39.716 --> 00:01:45.186
The goal is simple to state and demanding to implement: keep data as data.

00:01:45.186 --> 00:01:51.045
JavaScript developers prevent injection by designing application boundaries so untrusted input cannot change command meaning,

00:01:51.045 --> 00:01:53.780
query structure, document structure, or executable logic.
